      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">Strategic Time</h1>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <div class="bg-oat-100 p-6 mb-12 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-ink-light">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">For people:</span> Strategic choice exists in a temporal zone—fast enough to pursue meaningful goals, slow enough to learn whether they were wise. Collapse that zone from either direction and you lose the ability to choose at all.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-light">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">For organizations:</span> Markets coordinate at transaction speed; strategic commitments validate over years; values evolve over generations. The mismatch is not a bug—it's where strategy lives. Organizations exist to protect this zone from two failure modes: signal-chasing that collapses into noise, and survival pressure that collapses into mere persistence.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink-light text-sm pt-2 border-t border-oat-300">
          If these questions resonate, <a href="/contact" class="text-sage-700 hover:text-sage-900 underline">I'd welcome the conversation</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg">
        <p>A pharmaceutical company commits to a decade-long research program. Two years in, quarterly earnings disappoint. The stock drops 15%. Analysts ask why resources are tied up in projects with no near-term revenue. The research is sound; the timeline is appropriate for the science. But the market's clock ticks in quarters, and the research clock ticks in years. Neither is wrong about time. They simply measure different things.</p>

        <h2>Three Clocks</h2>

        <p>Economic activity operates across three temporal scales that are fundamentally mismatched. At the <strong>transaction level</strong>, prices clear in milliseconds to seconds—an instantaneous snapshot claiming to represent value. At the <strong>strategic level</strong>, commitments and capabilities unfold over years to decades, and assessment happens only in retrospect. At the <strong>evaluative level</strong>, the question of what counts as good evolves over decades to centuries, through cultural and institutional change that no one fully controls. The three clocks do not synchronize. They cannot synchronize. And this mismatch, rather than being a problem to solve, is precisely why strategic choice exists at all.</p>

        <p>If all questions could be resolved at transaction speed, there would be no strategy—only execution of what prices dictate. If no questions could ever be resolved, there would be no strategy either—only drift without learning. Strategy operates in the gap between these extremes: questions that are too slow to be answered by market signals but tractable enough that evidence eventually accumulates. I find this framing clarifying because it locates strategy not in any decision or capability but in a temporal property of the decision environment—the speed at which validation occurs relative to the speed at which coordination signals update.</p>

        <p>Hayek demonstrated something profound about market coordination that bears on this temporal structure. Prices enable coordinated action without shared understanding—the shipper need not know why tin prices rose, only that the price moved and what that implies for action. "The most significant fact about this system," Hayek wrote, "is the economy of knowledge with which it operates, or how little the individual participants need to know in order to be able to take the right action." But "the right action" presupposes an evaluative framework that price signals cannot generate. Markets coordinate brilliantly within boundaries they cannot determine—property rights, contract enforcement, what can be commodified, who counts as an actor. These boundaries stabilize at institutional speed, orders of magnitude slower than the price signals that operate within them. Hayek showed how to coordinate given objectives; the temporal implication is that the objectives within which coordination operates exist on a fundamentally different clock.</p>

        <h2>Market Degeneration</h2>

        <p>Two failure modes bound the space where strategy can operate. The first I think of as market degeneration—a compression of time. When a signal becomes important, we measure it more frequently. When we measure it more frequently, we make more decisions based on it. When we make more decisions based on it, the signal itself becomes the object of optimization rather than a proxy for something deeper.</p>

        <p>Nike's recent struggles illustrate how this unfolds. In 2015, the company pursued an aggressive direct-to-consumer transition. The unit economics were compelling: twice the revenue and three times the EBIT per shoe by cutting out wholesale intermediaries. They hired a tech CEO to accelerate digital capabilities, believing that quantitative customer data from apps would more than compensate for lost wholesale relationships. COVID appeared to vindicate the strategy—Nike's digital infrastructure let them capture share while competitors scrambled. DTC growth surged, margins expanded, and leadership doubled down by cutting more wholesale partners. At a 2021 analyst day, they projected mid-to-high-teens margins and low-double-digit growth. The stock hit its peak.</p>

        <p>Then came the reckoning. Nike lost significant share in performance running to Hoka and On—the very category that sustains the "best athlete" brand halo making their lifestyle products desirable. The cause: they had optimized for <em>observable</em> demand (app engagement, purchase data) while losing access to <em>latent</em> demand signals. Disbanded run clubs and severed wholesale relationships meant Nike didn't know what it didn't know. A product cycle that once took one year took four. They had confused <em>tractable</em> value creation—margin dollars per unit—with <em>generative</em> value creation, the brand credibility that makes people want the products in the first place. DTC captured more of the existing pie while eroding the mechanism that grew the pie. The shift from qualitative to quantitative feedback created a selection problem: they gained precision on known dimensions of customer behavior but lost sensitivity to emerging dimensions they weren't yet measuring.</p>

        <p>This pattern produces what I call market degeneration. The temporal horizon collapses toward zero—"value" becomes whatever the coordination mechanism says it is at any given instant. The evaluative content that should guide coordination proliferates into noise because there is no time for validation. Every signal is just another input to the next price.</p>

        <h2>Survival Degeneration</h2>

        <p>The opposite failure is equally destructive, and it operates by extending the temporal horizon to infinity rather than collapsing it to zero. When survival pressure dominates, the only question that matters is "are we still here?" and evaluative content collapses to binary existence. Organizations in survival mode make decisions that destroy their reason for existing in order to preserve their existence—they cut the capabilities that differentiate them, abandon the commitments that defined them, sacrifice the stakeholders who built them, all in service of continuing to be. A newspaper that fires its investigative journalists to cut costs may survive, but what remains worth preserving? An academic institution that abandons tenure to reduce fixed costs may balance its budget, but at what cost to the knowledge creation that justified its existence? By "substance" I do not mean profitability or market position—I mean the evaluative commitments that specify what the organization is <em>for</em>, the commitments that distinguish purposive action from mere persistence. The question "will we survive?" displaces the question "what are we for?"—and once survival becomes the only objective, there is nothing left to protect except the organization's own continuation.</p>

        <h2>The Strategic Zone</h2>

        <p>Between these failure modes lies what I call the strategic zone—the temporal band where the horizon is long enough to pursue rich objectives, not collapsed into market-speed repricing, and finite enough that validation can eventually occur, not extended into infinite survival. In the strategic zone, organizations can ask questions that matter: What should we be doing? For whom? By what standards? These questions have no instant answer, but they are not unanswerable. Evidence accumulates. Outcomes reveal themselves. Learning happens—just on timescales that exceed transaction-level coordination. The zone is not natural; it is institutionally constructed, and it must be actively maintained against pressures that would collapse it from either direction.</p>

        <p>Legitimacy operates as one of its key temporal buffers. Organizations survive partly by conforming to institutionalized expectations—the "gilded shelter" that protects them from immediate selection pressure. But that protection serves a purpose beyond mere survival: it allows strategic commitments to mature on their natural timescale rather than being compressed into market cycles or extended into pure persistence. Weak institutions push organizations toward survival degeneration because they cannot buffer existential pressure long enough for commitments to validate. Hyperliquid markets push toward market degeneration because signals update faster than the reality they track. Resisting market degeneration requires deliberate temporal architecture—long-term ownership structures, patient governance, metrics designed for slow validation, the discipline to not check signals more frequently than they can meaningfully change.</p>

        <h2>Why Firms Exist</h2>

        <p>This temporal structure reframes why firms exist. A firm can do something that a market cannot: commit. A firm can sustain a research program that will not yield returns for a decade. It can invest in employee development that builds capabilities over careers. It can promise customers product support for equipment with fifteen-year lifecycles. Markets struggle with these commitments because market signals update faster than validation can occur—a stock price that drops when quarterly earnings miss expectations penalizes commitments whose payoff horizon exceeds a quarter, even if those commitments are wise. The firm creates the temporal space where such commitments can exist, bridging the mismatch between market-speed coordination and slow-validating strategic purposes.</p>

        <p>I keep returning to the same question: what stops an organization from drifting? Each coordination cycle presents new information, new pressures, new signals. Without something stable, the organization migrates toward whatever its measurement systems make visible. What gets measured gets managed, and what gets managed comes to define purpose—even when no one intended that outcome. Purpose, when it works, resists this drift. Not aspiration—aspiration is too soft, too easily revised when circumstances shift. Purpose operates as a compression function that converts multidimensional, incommensurable values into tractable objectives around which coordination can occur. It says: these are the dimensions of value we commit to pursuing, for this duration, revised only under these conditions. Purpose does not eliminate the need to respond to circumstances; it specifies what kind of circumstances should trigger what kind of response—distinguishing operational adaptation from strategic revision from fundamental reconstitution. Without it, every signal is equally compelling. With it, some signals matter and others are noise.</p>

        <h2>The Design Problem</h2>

        <p>If the strategic zone is institutionally constructed, then maintaining it is a design problem—and most organizations do not think about it explicitly. Against market degeneration, they need mechanisms that protect slow-validating commitments from fast-cycle pressure: long-term ownership structures, governance that weights patient stakeholders, metrics designed to track what actually matters rather than what can be measured fastest, the discipline to not check signals more frequently than they can meaningfully change. Against survival degeneration, they need mechanisms that preserve evaluative richness even when resources are scarce: clear articulation of purpose that transcends mere existence, governance that asks not just "will we survive?" but "is survival worthwhile on these terms?"</p>

        <p>Most organizations drift toward one failure mode or the other. They chase metrics until the metrics become the mission, or they defend their existence until existence is all that remains to defend. The ones that avoid both do so through institutions that protect the temporal middle—the zone where strategic choice operates, where questions are slow enough to be meaningful and fast enough to be answerable. Whether any organization can maintain this balance indefinitely, rather than merely postponing one degeneration or the other, strikes me as genuinely open.</p>

        <p><em>Strategic choice is not given. It is constructed—and it must be defended against the pressures that would collapse it from both sides.</em></p>
      </div>

