          <li id="fn1">The three temporal levels I describe—transaction, strategic, and evaluative—are my synthesis of observations from financial economics (on market microstructure), strategic management (on commitment and competitive advantage), and institutional theory (on cultural and normative change). The key insight is that these operate on different clocks that cannot be synchronized.</li>
          <li id="fn2">The distinction between market-speed coordination and evaluative frameworks that markets cannot generate is developed more fully in "The Right Direction."</li>
          <li id="fn3">The Nike case is reconstructed from public sources including earnings calls, analyst reports, and industry coverage between 2015-2024. The pattern—optimizing for tractable metrics while losing generative capabilities—is common enough to deserve a name. I call it "market degeneration."</li>
          <li id="fn4">The concept of "gilded shelter" is my term for how legitimacy creates temporal protection for organizations. It builds on institutional theory, particularly Meyer and Rowan (1977) on institutionalized organizations, but extends the insight to temporal dynamics.</li>
          <li id="fn5">The pattern of maintaining commitment through apparent disadvantage—holding purpose while navigating survival pressure—appears across many successful transformations. I discuss this more fully through the T-Mobile example in "The Architecture of Commitment."</li>
